		'Reviewed commands.ts, tools.ts, native-tool.ts, approval.ts, BASH/EDIT/READ/READ_ENTRY/GREP/COMMAND, file-changes.ts, file-text.ts, tool-output.ts and tool-presentation.ts. Ownership: native-tool resolves complete calls before approvals; approval owns the best-effort safety decision; tools owns round ordering, error results, durable images and bounded output; fileChanges owns snapshots and lock release; BASH owns actual process lifetime and EDIT owns atomic planning and rollback. No dispatcher/parser merge: command loading isolates failures while tool registration and tool-specific syntax have different contracts.',
		'Candidates: (1) reject shared READ/READ_ENTRY batch aggregator: different successful headers and opposite image-source precedence need policy callbacks/options, while an image-only helper leaves coordination at callers; keep both bounded loops. (2) reject one BASH/EDIT resource wrapper: background process completion and EDIT rollback/recovery have distinct lifetimes. (3) reject cosmetic COMMAND Promise catch deletion as no meaningful ownership improvement. (4) accept unconditional release in fileChanges.finish, implementation b1g. Compared caller-side defensive release with owner-side nested finally; choose the latter, preserving ordering and removing the need for caller knowledge of commit-hook failures. Built-in commits.finish catches ordinary failures, but plugin wrappers can reject.'
